WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.900
In the last video, you saw the notation we'll use to define sequence learning problems.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.300
Now let's talk about how you can build a model, build a neural network to learn the mapping

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:11.300
from X to Y.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:17.360
Now, one thing you could do is try to use a standard neural network for this task.

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:22.080
So in our previous example, we had nine input words.

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:27.600
So you could imagine trying to take these nine input words, maybe the nine one-hot

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:33.520
vectors and feeding them into a standard neural network, maybe a few hidden layers, and then

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:39.540
eventually have this output, the nine values, zero or one, that tell you whether each word

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.620
is part of a person's name.

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.940
But this turns out not to work well.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.480
And there are really two main problems with this.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.700
The first is that the inputs and outputs can be different lengths and different examples.

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:58.300
So it's not as if every single example had the same input length, Tx, or the same output

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.320
length, Ty.

00:01:00.320 --> 00:01:06.440
And maybe if every sentence had a maximum length, maybe you could pad or zero pad every input

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:11.880
up to that maximum length, but this still doesn't seem like a good representation.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:16.560
And then the second, and maybe more serious problem, is that a naive neural network

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:20.880
on a architecture like this, it doesn't share features learned across different positions

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.260
of text.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:27.660
In particular, if the neural network has learned that maybe the words Harry appearing in position

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:33.960
one gives a sign that that's part of a person's name, then one would be nice if it automatically

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:40.060
figures out that Harry appearing in some other position, xt, also means that that might

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.060
be a person's name.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:47.360
And this is maybe similar to what you saw in convolutional neural networks, where you

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:52.560
want things learned for one part of the image to generalize quickly to other parts of the

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:58.000
image, and we like similar effects for sequence data as well.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.780
And similar to what you saw with confinets, using a better representation will also let

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:06.880
you reduce the number of parameters in your model.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:13.060
So previously, we said that each of these is a 10,000 dimensional one-hot vector.

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:17.120
And so this is just a very large input layer.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:23.600
If the total input size was, you know, maximum number of words times 10,000, and the weight

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:28.440
matrix of this first layer would end up having an enormous number of parameters.

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.280
So a recurrent neural network, which we'll start to describe in the next slide, does

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:37.180
not have either of these disadvantages.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:40.600
So what is a recurrent neural network?

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.560
Let's build one up.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:49.080
So if you are reading the sentence from left to right, the first word you read is the first

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:51.160
word, say, x1.

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:56.240
And what we're going to do is take the first word and feed it into a neural network layer.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.240
I'm going to draw it like this.

00:02:59.240 --> 00:03:02.840
So that's a hidden layer of the first neural network.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:07.420
And we could have the neural network maybe try to predict the output.

00:03:07.420 --> 00:03:10.520
So is this part of a person's name or not?

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:18.840
And what a recurrent neural network does is when it then goes on to read the second word

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:28.840
in the sentence, say, x2, instead of just predicting y2 using only x2, it also gets to

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:33.640
input some information from what had computed at time step one.

00:03:33.640 --> 00:03:39.880
So in particular, the activation value from time step one is passed on to time step two.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:50.500
And then at the next time step, a recurrent neural network inputs the third word, x3, and

00:03:50.500 --> 00:04:03.180
it tries to predict output some prediction, y hat three, and so on, up until the last

00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:13.640
time step where it inputs x, t, x, and then it outputs y hat, t, y.

00:04:13.720 --> 00:04:19.080
At least in this example, tx is equal to ty, and the architecture will change a bit if

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:22.360
tx and ty are not identical.

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:29.160
And so at each time step, the recurrent neural network passes on as activation to the next

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:31.640
time step for it to use.

00:04:31.640 --> 00:04:38.080
And to kick off the whole thing, we'll also have some made up activation at time zero.

00:04:38.080 --> 00:04:41.200
This is usually the vector of zeros.

00:04:41.200 --> 00:04:44.040
Some researchers will initialize a zero randomly.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:49.240
You have other ways to initialize a zero, but really having a vector of zeros as the

00:04:49.240 --> 00:04:54.360
fake time zero activation is the most common choice.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:56.680
And so that gets input to the neural network.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:02.880
In some research papers or in some books, you see this type of neural network drawn

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:10.800
with the following diagram in which at every time step, you input x and output y hat,

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:14.680
maybe sometimes there'll be a t index there.

00:05:14.680 --> 00:05:19.680
And then to denote the recurrent connection, sometimes people will draw a loop like that.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.320
The layer feeds back to itself.

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:27.320
Sometimes they'll draw a shaded box to denote that this is the shaded box here,

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.000
denotes a time delay of one step.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.600
I personally find these recurrent diagrams much harder to interpret.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:38.320
And so throughout this course, I'll tend to draw the unrolled diagram like the one

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:39.560
you have on the left.

00:05:39.560 --> 00:05:43.000
But if you see something like the diagram on the right in a textbook or in a

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:46.600
research paper, what it really means or the way I tend to think about it is to

00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:50.800
mentally unroll it into the diagram you have on the left instead.

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:55.560
The recurrent neural network scans through the data from left to right.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.520
And the parameters it uses for each time step are shared.

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.400
So there'll be a set of parameters, which we'll describe in greater detail

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:04.640
on the next slide.

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:09.120
But the parameters governing the connection from x1 to the hidden layer

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:12.960
will be some set of parameters we're going to write as wax.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:19.080
And it's the same parameters, wax, that it uses for every time step.

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.800
I guess we can write wax there as well.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:25.280
And the activations, the horizontal connections,

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.760
will be governed by some set of parameters, waa.

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:35.160
And it's the same parameters, waa, used on every time step.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:44.670
And similarly, there's some waa that governs the output predictions.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:48.790
And I'll describe on the next slide exactly how these parameters work.

00:06:48.790 --> 00:06:52.950
So in this recurrent neural network, what this means is that when making

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:58.470
the prediction for y3, it gets the information not only from x3,

00:06:58.470 --> 00:07:03.110
but also the information from x1 and x2, because the information from x1

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:08.150
can pass through this way to help the prediction with y3.

00:07:08.150 --> 00:07:11.030
Now, one weakness of this RNN is that it only

00:07:11.030 --> 00:07:15.630
uses the information that is earlier in the sequence to make a prediction.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:21.550
In particular, when predicting y3, it doesn't use information about the words

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:24.230
x4, x5, x6, and so on.

00:07:24.230 --> 00:07:29.870
And so this is a problem, because if you're given a sentence,

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:32.630
he said Teddy Roosevelt was a great president.

00:07:32.630 --> 00:07:37.030
In order to decide whether or not the word Teddy is part of a person's name,

00:07:37.030 --> 00:07:41.190
it'd be really useful to know not just information from the first two words,

00:07:41.190 --> 00:07:44.910
but to know information from the later words in the sentence as well.

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:49.550
Because the sentence could also have been, he said Teddy bears are on sale.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:52.110
And so given just the first three words,

00:07:52.110 --> 00:07:57.150
it's not possible to know for sure whether the word Teddy is part of a person's name.

00:07:57.150 --> 00:07:58.590
In the first example, it is.

00:07:58.590 --> 00:08:00.270
In the second example, it's not.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:05.870
But you can't tell the difference if you look only at the first three words.

00:08:05.870 --> 00:08:09.070
So one limitation of this particular neural network structure

00:08:09.070 --> 00:08:14.990
is that the prediction at a certain time uses inputs or uses information

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:19.710
from the inputs earlier in the sequence, but not information later in the sequence.

00:08:19.710 --> 00:08:24.430
We will address this in a later video where we talk about bi-directional

00:08:24.430 --> 00:08:29.190
recurrent neural networks, or BRNNs.

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:35.350
But for now, this simpler unidirectional neural network architecture

00:08:35.350 --> 00:08:38.670
will suffice to explain the key concepts.

00:08:38.670 --> 00:08:41.830
And we just have to make a quick modification to these ideas later

00:08:41.830 --> 00:08:46.470
to enable, say, the prediction of Y hat 3 to use both information earlier

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.830
in the sequence as well as information later in the sequence.

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.390
We'll get to that in a later video.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:55.350
So let's not write it explicitly what are the calculations

00:08:55.350 --> 00:08:57.830
that this neural network does.

00:08:57.830 --> 00:09:02.270
Here's a cleaned up version of the picture of the neural network.

00:09:02.270 --> 00:09:06.550
As I mentioned previously, typically you start it off with the input a0

00:09:06.550 --> 00:09:10.270
equals the vector of all zeros.

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:13.990
Next, this is what forward propagation looks like.

00:09:13.990 --> 00:09:20.030
To compute a1, you would compute that as an activation function,

00:09:20.030 --> 00:09:34.710
g applied to waa times a0 plus wax times x1 plus a bias,

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:36.870
which we're going to write as ba.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:42.550
And then to compute Y hat 1, the prediction in time set 1,

00:09:42.550 --> 00:09:47.310
that would be some activation function, maybe a different activation function

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:58.910
than the one above, but applied to wya times a1 plus by.

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:00.510
And the notation convention I'm going

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:02.990
to use for the substrate of these matrices,

00:10:02.990 --> 00:10:06.230
like that example, wax.

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:08.950
The second index means that this wax is going

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:12.190
to be multiplied by some x-like quantity.

00:10:12.190 --> 00:10:14.350
And this a means that this is used

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:18.230
to compute some a-like quantity, like so.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:25.270
And similarly, you notice that here, wya is multiplied by some a-like quantity

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:29.710
to compute a y-type quantity.

00:10:29.710 --> 00:10:35.270
The activation function used to compute the activations

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:39.430
will often be a tanh in the choice of an RNN.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:42.110
And sometimes, red loos are also used,

00:10:42.110 --> 00:10:48.590
although there's a, although the tanh is actually a pretty common choice.

00:10:48.590 --> 00:10:53.870
And we have other ways of preventing the vanishing gradient problem,

00:10:53.870 --> 00:10:56.430
which we'll talk about later this week.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:59.710
And depending on what your output y is,

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:02.390
if it is a binary classification problem,

00:11:02.390 --> 00:11:05.790
then I guess you would use a sigmoid activation function,

00:11:05.790 --> 00:11:09.430
or it could be a softmax if you have a k-way classification problem.

00:11:09.430 --> 00:11:11.710
But the choice of activation function here

00:11:11.710 --> 00:11:15.310
would depend on what type of output y you have.

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:19.310
So for the name entity recognition task, where y was either 0, 1,

00:11:19.310 --> 00:11:24.590
I guess the second g could be a sigmoid activation function.

00:11:24.590 --> 00:11:26.790
And, and I guess you could write, you know, g2,

00:11:26.790 --> 00:11:28.630
you wanna distinguish that this is a,

00:11:28.630 --> 00:11:30.630
these could be different activation functions,

00:11:30.630 --> 00:11:32.590
but I usually won't do that.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.390
And then more generally, at time t,

00:11:35.390 --> 00:11:44.110
at will be g of waa times a from the previous time step,

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:49.710
plus wax of x from the current time step, plus ba.

00:11:49.710 --> 00:11:54.470
And y hat t is equal to g, again,

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.030
could be different activation functions,

00:11:56.030 --> 00:12:04.030
but g of wya times at plus by.

00:12:04.030 --> 00:12:08.270
So these equations define forward propagation in the neural network,

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:12.070
where you would start off with a0 as a vector of all zeros,

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:17.270
and then using a0 and x1, you would compute a1 and y hat 1,

00:12:17.270 --> 00:12:24.870
and then you take x2 and use x2 and a1 to compute a2 and y hat 2,

00:12:24.870 --> 00:12:27.910
and so on, and you carry out forward propagation

00:12:27.910 --> 00:12:30.710
going from the left to the right of this picture.

00:12:30.710 --> 00:12:34.550
Now, in order to help us develop the more complex neural networks,

00:12:34.550 --> 00:12:38.950
I'm actually gonna take this notation and simplify it a little bit.

00:12:38.950 --> 00:12:43.590
So let me copy these two equations to the next slide.

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:44.990
Right, here they are.

00:12:44.990 --> 00:12:48.950
Um, and what I'm going to do is actually take,

00:12:48.950 --> 00:12:50.750
so to simplify the notation a bit,

00:12:50.750 --> 00:12:55.710
I'm actually gonna take that and write it in a slightly simpler way.

00:12:55.710 --> 00:13:01.510
Um, and so I'm gonna write this as at equals g times just a matrix,

00:13:01.510 --> 00:13:04.710
wa times a new quantity,

00:13:04.710 --> 00:13:09.350
which is going to be at minus 1, comma,

00:13:09.350 --> 00:13:16.110
xt and then plus ba.

00:13:16.110 --> 00:13:22.830
And so that underlying quantity on the left and right are supposed to be equivalent.

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:30.110
So the way we define wa is we'll take this matrix waa and this matrix wax

00:13:30.110 --> 00:13:36.230
and, um, put them side by side and stack them horizontally as follows,

00:13:36.230 --> 00:13:39.670
and this will be the matrix wa.

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:47.390
So for example, if a was a 100 dimensional,

00:13:47.390 --> 00:13:51.910
and in our running example, x was 10,000 dimensional,

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:57.510
then waa would have been a 100 by 100 dimensional matrix,

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:03.790
and wax would have been a 100 by 10,000 dimensional matrix.

00:14:03.790 --> 00:14:06.310
And so stacking these two matrices together,

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:08.950
this would be 100 dimensional,

00:14:08.950 --> 00:14:10.830
this would be 100,

00:14:10.830 --> 00:14:14.030
and this would be, I guess, 10,000 elements.

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:18.230
So wa will be a 100 by, um,

00:14:18.470 --> 00:14:22.110
10100 dimensional matrix.

00:14:22.110 --> 00:14:25.670
Right. I guess this diagram on the left is not drawn to scale.

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:29.150
Right. Since wax would be a very wide matrix.

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:36.630
And what this notation means is to just take the two vectors and stack them together.

00:14:36.630 --> 00:14:41.950
So I'm gonna use that notation to denote that we're going to take the vector at minus 1.

00:14:41.950 --> 00:14:46.950
So that's a 100 dimensional and stack it on top of

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:55.310
at. So this ends up being a 10100 dimensional vector.

00:14:55.310 --> 00:15:02.150
And so hopefully you can check for yourself that this matrix times this vector

00:15:02.150 --> 00:15:05.750
just gives you back the original quantity.

00:15:05.750 --> 00:15:12.230
Right. Because now this matrix waa times wax

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:17.910
multiplied by this a t minus 1 x t vector.

00:15:17.910 --> 00:15:27.830
This is just equal to waa times a t minus 1 plus wax times x t,

00:15:27.830 --> 00:15:32.270
which is exactly what we had back over here.

00:15:32.270 --> 00:15:36.230
So the advantage of this notation is that rather than carrying around

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:40.030
two parameter matrices waa and wax,

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:44.110
we can compress them into just one parameter matrix waa.

00:15:44.110 --> 00:15:48.830
And this will simplify our notation for when we develop more complex models.

00:15:48.830 --> 00:15:51.990
And then for this in a similar way, um,

00:15:51.990 --> 00:15:54.110
I'm just gonna rewrite this slightly.

00:15:54.110 --> 00:16:00.270
I'm gonna write this as wy at plus by.

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:04.390
And now we just have the subscripts in the notation,

00:16:04.390 --> 00:16:06.230
wy and by,

00:16:06.230 --> 00:16:09.150
it denotes what type of output quantity we're computing.

00:16:09.150 --> 00:16:13.550
So wy indicates that that's a weight matrix of computing a wide-light quantity.

00:16:13.550 --> 00:16:19.190
And here wa and ba on top indicates what those are the parameters for computing,

00:16:19.190 --> 00:16:22.590
you know, like an a, an activation output quantity.

00:16:22.590 --> 00:16:26.830
So that's it. You now know what is a basic recurrent neural network.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:31.030
Next, let's talk about back propagation and how you would learn with these RNNs.
